WEBVTT

00:00:00.324 --> 00:00:03.047
Mia, lemons and vinegar contain acids.

00:00:03.319 --> 00:00:07.930
Does that mean the best way to identify an
unknown laboratory liquid is a tiny taste?

00:00:08.437 --> 00:00:09.667
Definitely not.

00:00:10.014 --> 00:00:13.283
Familiar food examples are not
permission to taste chemicals.

00:00:14.081 --> 00:00:14.815
Good start.

00:00:15.312 --> 00:00:18.323
Acids produce hydrogen
ions in aqueous solution.

00:00:18.767 --> 00:00:20.810
Aqueous means dissolved in water.

00:00:21.226 --> 00:00:25.107
Citric acid occurs in lemons,
ethanoic acid in vinegar, and

00:00:25.109 --> 00:00:28.423
hydrochloric acid helps provide
acidic conditions in the stomach.

00:00:29.197 --> 00:00:29.924
And a base

00:00:29.982 --> 00:00:30.174
can

00:00:30.180 --> 00:00:30.823
neutralise an

00:00:30.832 --> 00:00:31.302
acid.

00:00:31.829 --> 00:00:33.525
Is every base an alkali?

00:00:34.343 --> 00:00:34.715
No.

00:00:35.125 --> 00:00:37.621
An alkali is a base
that dissolves in water.

00:00:37.857 --> 00:00:39.899
Sodium hydroxide is an alkali.

00:00:40.225 --> 00:00:44.658
Copper oxide is a base but is insoluble
in water, so it is not an alkali.

00:00:45.429 --> 00:00:49.004
All alkalis are bases, but
not all bases are alkalis.

00:00:49.452 --> 00:00:53.491
Like all squares are rectangles,
but not every rectangle is a square.

00:00:54.306 --> 00:00:55.146
Exactly.

00:00:55.539 --> 00:00:59.229
Acidic, alkaline, and neutral
describe the solution's behavior.

00:00:59.612 --> 00:01:03.490
We use indicators or a pH
meter, not taste or touch.

00:01:03.975 --> 00:01:07.149
Some alkalis are dangerously
corrosive, even though the

00:01:07.151 --> 00:01:08.977
word acid gets more attention.

00:01:09.862 --> 00:01:13.087
The dangerous one is not always
the one with the scariest name.

00:01:13.883 --> 00:01:14.425
Correct.

00:01:14.680 --> 00:01:18.699
Hazards depend on the chemical,
concentration, amount, and exposure.

00:01:19.130 --> 00:01:23.310
Wear the protection specified by your
teacher, follow the risk assessment, and

00:01:23.310 --> 00:01:25.563
keep chemicals away from skin and eyes.

00:01:25.960 --> 00:01:29.191
Never mix household cleaners
to investigate reactions.

00:01:29.945 --> 00:01:32.666
Our video can use diagrams and safe props.

00:01:33.190 --> 00:01:36.600
The real experiments belong in
a properly supervised laboratory

00:01:36.881 --> 00:01:41.664
On the usual school pH scale, acidic
solutions are below seven, neutral is

00:01:41.664 --> 00:01:46.048
around seven at ordinary room conditions,
and alkaline solutions are above seven.

00:01:46.396 --> 00:01:48.457
Lower pH means greater acidity.

00:01:49.300 --> 00:01:53.170
A pH three solution is only one
step more acidic than pH four.

00:01:53.491 --> 00:01:55.135
Does that mean only a tiny difference?

00:01:55.923 --> 00:02:00.661
In the introductory concentration
model, each pH step represents a tenfold

00:02:00.661 --> 00:02:02.942
change in hydrogen ion concentration.

00:02:03.439 --> 00:02:10.020
pH three is ten times pH four's hydrogen
ion concentration and 100 times pH five's.

00:02:10.763 --> 00:02:12.647
So the numbers are not a simple ruler.

00:02:13.106 --> 00:02:16.388
A change of two means a
factor of 100, not two.

00:02:17.188 --> 00:02:17.741
Right.

00:02:18.123 --> 00:02:21.892
The zero to 14 scale covers
many ordinary school solutions,

00:02:22.250 --> 00:02:25.540
but it is not an absolute limit
for every possible solution.

00:02:26.013 --> 00:02:30.823
Neutral pH also depends on temperature,
so we keep our classroom conditions clear.

00:02:31.628 --> 00:02:34.549
Litmus gives the exact pH, yes?

00:02:35.275 --> 00:02:36.060
Not exact.

00:02:36.484 --> 00:02:40.689
Blue litmus turns red in acid, and
red litmus turns blue in alkali.

00:02:41.347 --> 00:02:45.033
Universal indicator gives a range
of colors that estimates pH when

00:02:45.033 --> 00:02:46.680
compared with its supplied chart.

00:02:46.987 --> 00:02:52.008
Neutral solutions do not make red litmus
turn blue or blue litmus turn red.

00:02:52.802 --> 00:02:57.002
Typical universal indicator goes
red, orange, and yellow on the acidic

00:02:57.002 --> 00:03:01.789
side, green near neutral, then blue
and purple on the alkaline side.

00:03:02.602 --> 00:03:07.056
Yes, but compare with the actual chart
rather than assuming every product

00:03:07.056 --> 00:03:08.898
uses identical color boundaries.

00:03:09.414 --> 00:03:13.079
Red cabbage also contains
natural pH-sensitive pigments.

00:03:13.484 --> 00:03:17.723
A pH meter gives a numerical reading
when used and calibrated appropriately.

00:03:18.502 --> 00:03:23.487
Color is evidence about pH, not
a label saying safe to drink.

00:03:23.839 --> 00:03:26.574
What does concentrated
mean compared with dilute?

00:03:27.377 --> 00:03:30.509
More acid per volume of
solution rather than less.

00:03:31.091 --> 00:03:33.556
Strong means the same thing, doesn't it?

00:03:34.332 --> 00:03:35.258
That is the trap.

00:03:35.787 --> 00:03:38.844
A strong acid ionizes
essentially completely in water.

00:03:39.207 --> 00:03:41.377
A weak acid ionizes only partly.

00:03:41.762 --> 00:03:44.766
Concentration tells us how much
acid is present per volume.

00:03:45.032 --> 00:03:47.560
Strength describes its tendency to ionize.

00:03:48.354 --> 00:03:52.461
So we can have a dilute strong
acid or a concentrated weak acid.

00:03:53.069 --> 00:03:55.409
The two labels answer different questions.

00:03:56.065 --> 00:03:56.910
Exactly.

00:03:57.234 --> 00:04:02.029
The pH depends on both the acid's behavior
and concentration, among other conditions.

00:04:02.388 --> 00:04:05.571
A pH reading alone does not
identify whether the acid is

00:04:05.573 --> 00:04:07.135
chemically strong or weak.

00:04:08.014 --> 00:04:11.431
My imaginary marking pen nearly
labeled weak as harmless.

00:04:11.745 --> 00:04:12.950
I should rescue that too.

00:04:13.792 --> 00:04:14.470
Please do.

00:04:15.011 --> 00:04:18.667
A weak acid can still be hazardous,
especially when concentrated.

00:04:19.063 --> 00:04:21.958
Even dilute solutions need
appropriate precautions.

00:04:22.335 --> 00:04:26.718
We do not decide handling safety from
a single word, such as weak or natural.

00:04:27.412 --> 00:04:30.567
And concentration needs to be
controlled when comparing how

00:04:30.580 --> 00:04:34.537
different metals react with an
acid, otherwise the test is unfair.

00:04:35.237 --> 00:04:35.786
Correct.

00:04:36.059 --> 00:04:40.080
Keep the acid identity, concentration,
volume, and temperature comparable, and
